   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 📝 Arithmetic Operators in Python\n",
    "\n",
    "| Operator         | Symbol | Example      | Description                       | Result |\n",
    "|------------------|--------|--------------|-----------------------------------|--------|\n",
    "| Addition         | `+`    | `5 + 2`      | Adds two numbers                  | `7`    |\n",
    "| Subtraction      | `-`    | `5 - 2`      | Subtracts second from first       | `3`    |\n",
    "| Multiplication   | `*`    | `5 * 2`      | Multiplies two numbers            | `10`   |\n",
    "| Division         | `/`    | `5 / 2`      | Divides and returns float result  | `2.5`  |\n",
    "| Floor Division   | `//`   | `5 // 2`     | Returns integer part only         | `2`    |\n",
    "| Modulus          | `%`    | `5 % 2`      | Returns the remainder             | `1`    |\n",
    "| Exponentiation   | `**`   | `5 ** 2`     | Raises to the power               | `25`   |\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fc2dcfe1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ce8faab5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 🖊️ Assignment Operators in Python\n",
    "\n",
    "| Operator | Example     | Same As        | Description                            |\n",
    "|----------|-------------|----------------|----------------------------------------|\n",
    "| `=`      | `x = 5`     | —              | Assigns value 5 to `x`                 |\n",
    "| `+=`     | `x += 3`    | `x = x + 3`    | Adds 3 to `x`                          |\n",
    "| `-=`     | `x -= 2`    | `x = x - 2`    | Subtracts 2 from `x`                   |\n",
    "| `*=`     | `x *= 4`    | `x = x * 4`    | Multiplies `x` by 4                   |\n",
    "| `/=`     | `x /= 2`    | `x = x / 2`    | Divides `x` by 2 (float result)       |\n",
    "| `//=`    | `x //= 2`   | `x = x // 2`   | Floor divides `x`                     |\n",
    "| `%=`     | `x %= 2`    | `x = x % 2`    | Assigns remainder to `x`              |\n",
    "| `**=`    | `x **= 2`   | `x = x ** 2`   | Raises `x` to the power of 2          |\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "149fb282",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2ee11ad7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 🔍 Comparison Operators in Python\n",
    "\n",
    "Comparison operators compare two values and return either `True` or `False`.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Operator | Example       | Description                      | Result (if `x = 5`, `y = 3`) |\n",
    "|----------|---------------|----------------------------------|------------------------------|\n",
    "| `==`     | `x == y`      | Equal to                         | `False`                      |\n",
    "| `!=`     | `x != y`      | Not equal to                     | `True`                       |\n",
    "| `>`      | `x > y`       | Greater than                     | `True`                       |\n",
    "| `<`      | `x < y`       | Less than                        | `False`                      |\n",
    "| `>=`     | `x >= y`      | Greater than or equal to         | `True`                       |\n",
    "| `<=`     | `x <= y`      | Less than or equal to            | `False`                      |\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d8ae4493",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "66e28147",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 🤔 Logical Operators in Python\n",
    "\n",
    "Logical operators are used to combine conditional statements.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Operator | Example             | Description                                 | Result (if `x = 5`, `y = 3`) |\n",
    "|----------|---------------------|---------------------------------------------|------------------------------|\n",
    "| `and`    | `(x > 2 and y < 5)` | Returns `True` if both conditions are true  | `True`                       |\n",
    "| `or`     | `(x > 10 or y < 5)` | Returns `True` if at least one is true      | `True`                       |\n",
    "| `not`    | `not(x > 2)`        | Reverses the result (True → False)          | `False`                      |\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "eae3886a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "76623ca9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 🧍 Identity Operators in Python\n",
    "\n",
    "Identity operators are used to compare **the memory location** of two objects.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Operator | Example      | Description                                         | Result (if `x = y = 5`) |\n",
    "|----------|--------------|-----------------------------------------------------|--------------------------|\n",
    "| `is`     | `x is y`     | True if both variables point to the same object     | `True`                   |\n",
    "| `is not` | `x is not y` | True if they point to different objects             | `False`                  |\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d63501bf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "860bae8c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 📦 Membership Operators in Python\n",
    "\n",
    "Membership operators check whether a value is present in a sequence (like a list, string, set, or tuple).\n",
    "\n",
    "| Operator | Example        | Description                                   | Result (if `x = [1, 2, 3]`) |\n",
    "|----------|----------------|-----------------------------------------------|-----------------------------|\n",
    "| `in`     | `2 in x`       | True if the value exists in the sequence      | `True`                      |\n",
    "| `not in` | `4 not in x`   | True if the value does not exist in the sequence | `True`                   |\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "60305cfc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "faad546b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 🧮 Bitwise Operators in Python\n",
    "\n",
    "Bitwise operators work on bits and perform operations bit by bit.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Operator | Name        | Example    | Description                                | Result (if `a = 5`, `b = 3`) |\n",
    "|----------|-------------|------------|--------------------------------------------|-------------------------------|\n",
    "| `&`      | AND         | `a & b`    | Bits that are 1 in both `a` and `b`        | `1`                           |\n",
    "| `|`      | OR          | `a | b`    | Bits that are 1 in either `a` or `b`       | `7`                           |\n",
    "| `^`      | XOR         | `a ^ b`    | Bits that are 1 in one but not both        | `6`                           |\n",
    "| `~`      | NOT         | `~a`       | Inverts all the bits of `a`                | `-6`                          |\n",
    "| `<<`     | Left Shift  | `a << 1`   | Shifts bits of `a` left by 1 (a × 2)       | `10`                          |\n",
    "| `>>`     | Right Shift | `a >> 1`   | Shifts bits of `a` right by 1 (a ÷ 2)      | `2`                           |\n"
     "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  }
